    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? It's just that goblins are notorious for their love of hiding in small, hard-to-reach places, and the capacitor is a perfect example of that. But, I digress.\n\nTo check if the capacitor is blown, you'll need to use a multimeter. Set the multimeter to the ohms function and touch the leads to the capacitor terminals. If the capacitor is blown, the multimeter will show an open circuit, indicated by a reading of infinity or a very high resistance value (usually above 1 megohm). \n\nVisually, a blown capacitor will typically show signs of physical damage, such as cracks, breaks, or burn marks on the ceramic or plastic casing. It might also be discolored or have a strange odor, similar to a goblin's lair after a fire – not a pleasant sight, I assure you.\n\nWhen checking the capacitor, make sure to turn off the power to the AC unit at the circuit breaker or fuse box. You don't want to risk getting zapped like a goblin caught in a lightning storm – not a good way to start your day, if you know what I mean.\n\nAlso",
